-names>RS</given-names></name><name><surname>Redfield</surname><given-names>RJ</given-names></name></person-group><year>1997</year><article-title>The hotspot conversion paradox and the evolution of meiotic recombination</article-title><source>Proc Natl Acad Sci U S A</source><volume>94</volume><fpage>8058</fpage><lpage>8063</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9223314</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pgen-0030035-b012"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Pineda-Krch</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Redfield</surname><given-names>RJ</given-names></name></person-group><year>2005</year><article-title>Persistence and loss of meiotic recombination hotspots</article-title><source>Genetics</source><volume>169</volume><fpage>2319</fpage><lpage>2333</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15687277</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pgen-0030035-b013"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Carrington</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Cullen</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name></person-group><year>2004</year><article-title>Justified chauvinism: Advances in defining meiotic recombination through sperm typing</article-title><source>Trends Genet</source><volume>20</volume><fpage>196</fpage><lpage>205</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15041174</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pgen-0030035-b014"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Jeffreys</surname><given-names>AJ</given-names></name><name><surname>Neumann</surname><given-names>R</given-names></name></person-group><year>2002</year><article-title>Reciprocal crossover asymmetry and meiotic drive in a human recombination hot spot</article-title><source>Nat Genet</source><volume>31</volume><fpage>267</fpage><lpage>271</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12089523</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pgen-0030035-b015"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Jeffreys</surname><given-names>AJ</given-names></name><name><surname>Neumann</surname><given-names>R</given-names></name></person-group><year>2005</year><article-title>Factors influencing recombination frequency and distribution in a human meiotic crossover hotspot</article-title><source>Hum Mol Genet</source><volume>14</volume><fpage>2277</fpage><lpage>2287</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15987698</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pgen-0030035-b016"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Jeffreys</surname><given-names>AJ</given-names></name><name><surname>Murray</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><name><surname>Neumann</surname><given-names>R</given-names></name></person-group><year>1998</year><article-title>High-resolution mapping of crossovers in human sperm defines a minisatellite-associated recombination hotspot</article-title><source>Mol Cell</source><volume>2</volume><fpage>267</fpage><lpage>273</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9734365</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pgen-0030035-b017"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Yauk</surname><given-names>CL</given-names></name><name><surname>Bois</surname><given-names>PRJ</given-names></name><name><surname>Jeffreys</surname><given-names>AJ</given-names></name></person-group><year>2003</year><article-title>High-resolution sperm typing of meiotic recombination in the mouse MHC <italic>E</italic><sub>&#x003b2;</sub> gene</article-title><source>EMBO J</source><volume>22</volume><fpage>1389</fpage><lpage>1397</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12628931</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pgen-0030035-b018"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Grimm</surname><given-names>C</given-names></name><name><surname>Schaer</surname><given-names>P</given-names></name><name><surname>Munz</surname><given-names>P</given-names></name><name><surname>Kohli</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name></person-group><year>1991</year><article-title>The strong adh1 promoter stimulates mitotic and meiotic recombination at the ade6 gene of <named-content content-type="genus-species">Schizosaccharomyces pombe</named-content></article-title><source>Mol Cell Biol</source><volume>11</volume><fpage>289</fpage><lpage>298</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">1986226</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pgen-0030035-b019"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Nag</surname><given-names>DK</given-names></name><name><surname>Kurst</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name></person-group><year>1997</year><article-title>A 140-bp-long palindromic sequence induces double-strand breaks during meiosis in the yeast <named-content content-type="genus-species">Saccharomyces cerevisiae</named-content></article-title><source>Genetics</source><volume>146</volume><fpage>835</fpage><lpage>847</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9215890</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pgen-0030035-b020"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Petes</surname><given-names>TD</given-names></name></person-group><year>2001</year><article-title>Meiotic recombination hot spots and cold spots</article-title><source>Nat Rev Genet</source><volume>2</volume><fpage>360</fpage><lpage>369</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11331902</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pgen-0030035-b021"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Szostak</surname><given-names>JW</given-names></name><name><surname>Orr-Weaver</surname><given-names>TL</given-names></name><name><surname>Rothstein</surname><given-names>RJ</given-names></name><name><surname>Stahl</surname><given-names>FW</given-names></name></person-group><year>1983</year><article-title>The double-strand-break repair model for recombination</article-title><source>Cell</source><volume>33</volume><fpage>25</fpage><lpage>35</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">6380756</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pgen-0030035-b022"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Gutz</surname><given-names>H</given-names></name><name><surname>Leslie</surname><given-names>JF</given-names></name></person-group><year>1976</year><article-title>Gene conversion: A hitherto overlooked parameter in population genetics</article-title><source>Genetics</source><volume>83</volume><fpage>861</fpage><lpage>866</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">971809</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pgen-0030035-b023"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Nagylaki</surname><given-names>T</given-names></name></person-group><year>1983</year><article-title>Evolution of a finite population under gene conversion</article-title><source>Proc Natl Acad Sci U S A</source><volume>80</volume><fpage>6278</fpage><lpage>6281</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">6578508</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pgen-0030035-b024"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Jeffreys</surname><given-names>AJ</given-names></name><name><surname>May</surname><given-names>CA</given-names></name></person-group><year>2004</year><article-title>Intense and highly localized gene conversion activity in human meiotic crossover hot spots</article-title><source>Nat Genet</source><volume>36</volume><fpage>151</fpage><lpage>156</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">14704667</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pgen-0030035-b025"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Nachman</surname><given-names>MW</given-names></name><name><surname>Crowell</surname><given-names>SL</given-names></name></person-group><year>2000</year><article-title>Estimate of the mutation rate per nucleotide in humans</article-title><source>Genetics</source><volume>156</volume><fpage>297</fpage><lpage>304</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10978293</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pgen-0030035-b026"><citation citation-type="journal"><collab>Chimpanzee Sequencing and Analysis Consortium</collab><year>2005</year><article-title>Initial sequence of the chimpanzee genome and comparison with the human genome</article-title><source>Nature</source><volume>437</volume><fpage>69</fpage><lpage>87</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">16136131</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pgen-0030035-b027"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Chen</surname><given-names>FC</given-names></name><name><surname>Li</surname><given-names>WH</given-names></name></person-group><year>2001</year><article-title>Genomic divergences between humans and other hominoids and the effective population size of the common ancestor of humans and chimpanzees</article-title><source>Am J Hum Genet</source><volume>68</volume><fpage>444</fpage><lpage>456</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11170892</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pgen-0030035-b028"><citation citation-type="book"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Takahata</surname><given-names>N</given-names></name><name><surname>Satta</surname><given-names>Y</given-names></name></person-group><year>2002</year><article-title>Modern developments in theoretical population genetics: The legacy of Gustave Mal&#x000e9;cot</article-title><person-group person-group-type="editor"><name><surname>Slatkin</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Veuille</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name></person-group><source>Pre-speciation coalescence and the effective size of ancestral populations</source><publisher-loc>Oxford</publisher-loc><publisher-name>Oxford University Press</publisher-name><fpage>52</fpage><lpage>71</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="pgen-0030035-b029"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-